# The sandbar release that drives this repo's own unattended series (#66).
#
# `npm run sandbar` installs exactly this into `.sandbar/driver/` and runs it.
# Nothing after that point is built from the working tree, so an operator can
# hold local commits and uncommitted edits while a series runs.
#
# One npm spec, `github:<owner>/<repo>#v<major>.<minor>.<patch>`. A TAG, as a
# consumer would pin: an untagged state is not pinnable and a sha is not a
# substitute for one.
#
# THE PIN LAGS THIS CHECKOUT, ALWAYS, and that is a rule rather than an
# accident. `auto-tag.yml` creates `vX.Y.Z` from package.json's version at the
# PUSHED HEAD, and the merger lands a whole source pass in one push, so a
# version that is not last in its pass never becomes a tag at all. Tags are
# therefore SPARSE relative to versions — a handful across a whole minor
# series of thirty-odd bumps, which `git tag --list 'v*'` beside `git log
# -- package.json` shows for any of them — and stating it as a ratio rather
# than a count is deliberate: the count moves every time a pass lands, the
# sparsity does not. The version being written in this checkout is therefore
# never installable: it has not landed,
# and even once it does it may not be the one that gets tagged. Pinning it
# would leave `npm install` failing on every launch and the loop stopping until
# a human intervened — the exact manual step #65 and #66 exist to delete.
# `launcher.test.ts` holds the rule as an assertion: the pinned version must be
# strictly older than package.json's, and must satisfy the `requiresSandbar`
# floor `sandbar.config.mjs` declares.
#
# So moving the pin is its own later commit, made deliberately once a version
# has landed, been tagged and been watched — that delay IS the feature, and it
# is what makes a self-hosted series behave like a consumer's. Iterating
# unlanded orchestrator code stays a `npm run build && node dist/cli.js` away,
# by hand.
#
# Nothing warns when THIS file is behind origin, and preflight's
# `staleConfigWarning` deliberately does not cover it even though the launcher
# reads it from the same unrefreshed checkout the config comes from. The two
# are not symmetric. A landed `gateStack` change is a decision about how
# branches are judged that somebody made FOR the series, and a run holding an
# older copy judges with a standard nobody chose — which is why it is worth a
# line on stdout. Moving the pin is the opposite: a deliberate commit whose
# whole point is that a human decided a release had been watched long enough,
# so the pin in the operator's checkout IS the driver they chose, and one they
# have not pulled is one they have not chosen yet. There is also nothing to
# warn from — the launcher runs before the driver exists and has no preflight,
# no cache and no fetch.
#
# Moving it forward: pick a version `git tag --list 'v*'` actually shows, and
# raise `requiresSandbar` in `sandbar.config.mjs` in the same commit as anything
# that file starts asking that newer sandbar for. Falling BACK to an older
# release is the same edit in reverse and is two edits, not one: lower
# `requiresSandbar` too, or the driver installs and then refuses the config it
# was installed to read.
#
# Lines starting with `#` are comments; exactly one spec line must remain.
github:Tojins/sandbar#v0.24.6
